# The callback receiver for the OBSERVER store's forgetting callbacks (hippocampus TODO 92).
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. Hippocampus' callbacks are a push feed of what a store has forgotten: one POST per
# deletion batch and one per sleep cycle, backed by a persisted queue with retry state, a bounded
# backlog and an abandon path. The interesting behaviour is all in conditions a test constructs
# artificially and a live demo produces for free - a receiver that goes away for an hour, a cycle
# that forgets thousands of memories at once, a restart mid-backoff. So the point of this container
# is that it EXISTS and can be taken away; what it does with a delivery is deliberately nothing.
#
# WHY CADDY AND NOT A PURPOSE-BUILT RECEIVER. A receiver that stored or rendered the feed would be a
# first-party image, which means a build, a release train and a deploy script, all to demonstrate
# something that is not the receiver's. `respond 204` is the whole contract the queue cares about,
# and caddy:2 is already a pinned third-party image on this host.
#
# WHY IT IS UNAUTHENTICATED. `callbacks.token` and `callbacks.signingSecret` exist and work, but
# neither would be verified here - Caddy can check a bearer header and cannot check an HMAC, so
# signing would mean sending a signature nobody validates. It is not published (no `ports:` in the
# compose file), it is reachable only from the `shared` container network, and it accepts a feed
# rather than serving one. A shared secret between two containers on one network would be theatre,
# and a mismatched one would present exactly as the receiver outage this is here to let us watch.
#
# WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM THE FRONT CADDY. The front Caddy could have carried this on an unpublished
# port with no compose edit at all. It is a separate container because stopping the receiver on its
# own is the single most interesting thing anyone will do with this - and on the front Caddy that
# would take the whole site down with it.
{
	# Nothing administers this and nothing reaches it over TLS: it is an internal sink on a container
	# network. auto_https off keeps Caddy from trying to provision a certificate for a bare port.
	admin off
	auto_https off
}

:8099 {
	# Access logging is what makes a delivery visible - `podman logs showcase_hippocampus-callback-sink_1`.
	# JSON because the useful part is structured: the request headers carry
	# X-Hippocampus-Callback-Kind, which names the delivery (memory_forgotten / event_forgotten /
	# sleep_completed) without the body being read.
	log {
		format json
	}

	# The compose healthcheck's target. Deliberately not the delivery path: a GET against /callbacks
	# would either have to be accepted (making the healthcheck indistinguishable from a delivery in
	# the log) or refused (making the healthcheck fail).
	handle /healthz {
		respond "ok" 200
	}

	# Every branch below is a `handle`, and that is not stylistic. Caddy sorts directives by TYPE, not
	# by the order they are written, so a `route` mixed in beside these would be tried after the
	# catch-all `handle` at the bottom and never match - which is exactly what an earlier draft of this
	# file did, answering 404 to every delivery while validating cleanly. `handle` blocks are mutually
	# exclusive and are evaluated in written order, so this reads the way it behaves.
	@delivery {
		path /callbacks
		method POST
	}

	# 204 rather than 200: the delivery is accepted and there is nothing to say about it. Any 2xx
	# clears the queue row; anything else defers it onto the backoff.
	handle @delivery {
		respond 204
	}

	handle /callbacks {
		respond "the callback sink accepts POST only" 405
	}

	handle {
		respond "not found" 404
	}
}
